%N A401263 Decimal expansion of the largest possible volume of an apex-split hexagonal bi-pyramid whose 9 vertices lie on a sphere with radius 1.
%C A401263 This polyhedron has 4 vertices of degree 4, 4 of degree 5, and one vertex of degree 6. See Hoehner and Landford for an illustration.
%C A401263 The adjacency list of its graph is given by 1[2 3 4 5 6 7], 2[1 7 8 3], 3[1 2 8 4], 4[1 3 8 9 5], 5[1 4 9 6], 6[1 5 9 7], 7[1 6 9 8 2], 8[2 7 9 4 3], 9[4 8 7 6 5].
%C A401263 No analytical representation of this volume is known. This is the second largest volume after the record-setting arrangement in the form of a tri-augmented triangular prism of any arrangement of 9 vertices on the surface of a sphere.
%H A401263 Steven Hoehner and Jeff Ledford, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.04392">The maximum volume polytope with nine vertices inscribed in the sphere</a>, arXiv:2608.04392 [math.MG], 5 Aug 2026 (Figure 6, page 28).
%F A401263 This volume is < 3*sqrt(6/5)/2 + sqrt(91)/24 = 2.040642339772559... and thus less than the global maximum A339261 = 2.04375011589963984..., see Hoehner and Landford, Theorem 4.6, page 28.
%e A401263 1.978381787196543414509105399741387191769125927...
